<td id="S4.T4.5.11.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.8822</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.5.12" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T4.5.12.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">Z</td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.12.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.1135</td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.12.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.0220</td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.12.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.1484</td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.12.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">0.9612</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T4.5.13" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T4.5.13.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">3D</td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.13.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">0.2515</td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.13.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">0.0812</td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.13.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">0.2850</td>
<td id="S4.T4.5.13.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_b">0.9479</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the DNN method, the mean absolute errors (MAEs) along the X, Y, and Z axes, and in the 3D space, are 0.1986 mm, 0.2336 mm, 0.1493 mm, and 0.3880 mm, respectively. Although GPSO-DNN improves on DNN with MAEs of 0.1588 mm (X), 0.1848 mm (Y), 0.1618 mm (Z) and 0.3405 mm (3D), its performance still lags behind SPI-BoTER, which achieves 0.1110 mm, 0.1537 mm, 0.1135 mm and 0.2515 mm for the corresponding dimensions. These results indicate that both DNN and GPSO-DNN exhibit greater variability and larger prediction errors in 3D coordinate estimation compared to SPI-BoTER.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">SPI-BoTER outperforms the other models in all four evaluation metrics. Its 3D MAE of 0.2515 mm represents a reduction of 35.16% compared to DNN and 26.14% compared to GPSO-DNN. Similarly, its 3D <math id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> score of 0.9479 shows an improvement of 7.87% and 4.51% over DNN and GPSO-DNN, respectively. The low MAE values across all axes (X: 0.1110 mm, Y: 0.1537 mm, Z: 0.1135 mm) further validate the stability of the SPI-BoTER predictions, demonstrating its effectiveness in suppressing anomalies in the 3D coordinate space.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">These experimental results indicate that the SPI-BoTER model, through its dual-branch architecture combining physical mechanisms and data-driven learning, along with a hybrid loss function, significantly enhances physical interpretability and robustness under extreme conditions. While maintaining the flexibility of DNN-based models, SPI-BoTER provides a more effective solution for compensating robotic arm positioning errors in small-sample scenarios.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.3 </span>Ablation Study on Loss Terms in SPI</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate the necessity of each loss term in the SPI, we performed an ablation study based on the BoTER model. The experimental group design is summarized in Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.2.3 Ablation Study on Loss Terms in SPI ‣ 4.2 Performance on the UR5 Industrial Robot Random Point Dataset ‣ 4 Results and Discussion ‣ SPI-BoTER: Error Compensation for Industrial Robots via Sparse Attention Masking and Hybrid Loss with Spatial-Physical Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 5</span>: </span><span id="S4.T5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Design of Ablation Groups for SPI Loss Terms</span></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T5.5" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T5.5.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T5.5.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T5.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Group Name</span></th>
<th id="S4.T5.5.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T5.5.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Modification</span></th>
<th id="S4.T5.5.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T5.5.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Objective</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T5.5.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T5.5.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Baseline</td>
<td id="S4.T5.5.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">All loss terms and adaptive parameters retained</td>
<td id="S4.T5.5.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Establish the performance benchmark</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T5.5.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.T5.5.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b">Data Only</td>
<td id="S4.T5.5.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b">Only retains <math id="S4.T5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>, spatial physics loss set to 0</td>
<td id="S4.T5.5.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_b">Test the necessity of physical constraints</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table <a href="#S4.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ 4.2.3 Ablation Study on Loss Terms in SPI ‣ 4.2 Performance on the UR5 Industrial Robot Random Point Dataset ‣ 4 Results and Discussion ‣ SPI-BoTER: Error Compensation for Industrial Robots via Sparse Attention Masking and Hybrid Loss with Spatial-Physical Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, the <span id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> baseline</span> group retains both the spatial physics loss term and the adaptive parameters to establish a benchmark for performance comparison. In contrast, the <span id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data Only</span> group retains only the residual loss of data <math id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> while setting the loss based on physics to zero, in order to assess the importance of physical constraints. The experimental results are illustrated in Figure <a href="#S4.F15" title="Figure 15 ‣ 4.2.3 Ablation Study on Loss Terms in SPI ‣ 4.2 Performance on the UR5 Industrial Robot Random Point Dataset ‣ 4 Results and Discussion ‣ SPI-BoTER: Error Compensation for Industrial Robots via Sparse Attention Masking and Hybrid Loss with Spatial-Physical Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> and detailed in Table <a href="#S4.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ 4.2.3 Ablation Study on Loss Terms in SPI ‣ 4.2 Performance on the UR5 Industrial Robot Random Point Dataset ‣ 4 Results and Discussion ‣ SPI-BoTER: Error Compensation for Industrial Robots via Sparse Attention Masking and Hybrid Loss with Spatial-Physical Information" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F15" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2506.22788v1/dataonly_baseline_comparison.png" id="S4.F15.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:286/199;" width="286" height="199" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F15.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 15</span>: </span><span id="S4.F15.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance Comparison of Ablation Groups for Each Loss Term of SPI</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S4.T6" class="ltx_table">
